## 19. Data Accuracy, Safety and Transparency

This is an educational software demonstration.

Follow these rules:

- Clearly label generated readings as SIMULATED DATA.
- Never present simulated health readings as actual astronaut measurements.
- Never claim physical sensors are connected.
- Never present simulated analytical performance as clinically validated.
- Never invent NASA requirements.
- Never invent medical or environmental safety limits.
- Distinguish established scientific information from proposed engineering solutions.
- Distinguish correlation from causation.
- Do not automatically diagnose medical conditions.
- Do not replace qualified medical personnel or approved mission procedures.
- Do not treat missing data as normal data.
- Do not hide uncertainty.
- Do not make confident conclusions from unreliable sensor readings.
- Ensure critical environmental safety findings remain detectable even when health information is incomplete.

Use credible NASA publications, scientific research and authoritative technical references when proposing sensor thresholds, exposure periods or analytical methods.

If a validated threshold is unavailable, use an explicitly labelled configurable demonstration value rather than presenting an invented value as an established safety limit.

